  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/assets/feb02396a635c3f416b07383b03269e12fde738da3345aa6d6d9fe5350745746.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a function $y=f(x)$ and its inverse $y={f}^{-1}(x).$ The point $A$ has coordinates $(1,0.5)$ and lies on $y=f(x).$ The point $B$ has coordinates $(0.5,1)$ and lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$ If ${f}^{\u2032}(1)=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}\u20614},$ what is $({f}^{-1})^{\u2032}(0.5)?$\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{\u2032}(0.5)=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "Since $B\\,(0.5,1)$ lies on ${f}^{-1},$ we have ${f}^{-1}(0.5)=1.$\nWe're also told that ${f}^{\u2032}(1)=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}\u20614}.$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse function is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{\u2032}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{\u2032}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=0.5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{\u2032}(0.5) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{\u2032}({f}^{-1}(0.5))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{\u2032}(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}\u20614})} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}\u20614.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
